        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 252,
        "headingId": 765,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>9120/0 Spindle cell haemangioma</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T12:00:29"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 823,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 766,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127786,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 252,
        "headingId": 766,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>2F2Y &amp; XH6RP8 Other specified benign cutaneous neoplasms &amp; Spindle cell haemangioma</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T12:00:29"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 824,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 767,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127787,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 252,
        "headingId": 767,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Not recommended:</em> spindle cell haemangioendothelioma (obsolete).</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T12:00:29"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 825,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 768,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127788,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 252,
        "headingId": 768,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T12:00:29"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 826,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 769,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127789,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 252,
        "headingId": 769,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>This lesion affects the distal extremities and occasionally the neck.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T12:00:29"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 827,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 770,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127790,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 252,
        "headingId": 770,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The lesions are multiple asymptomatic bluish nodules developing over many years, with little tendency for regression. There is a strong association with Maffucci syndrome, and less common associations with lymphoedema, early-onset varicose veins, and Klippel&ndash;Tr&eacute;naunay syndrome {7661276; 2071088; 23485734; 8827025}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T12:00:29"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 828,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 771,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127791,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 252,
        "headingId": 771,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>This rare haemangioma is more common in the second and third decades of life, with no sex predilection.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T12:00:29"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 829,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 772,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127792,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 252,
        "headingId": 772,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Spindle cell haemangioma is associated with Maffucci syndrome {7661276; 23485734}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T12:00:29"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 830,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 773,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127793,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 252,
        "headingId": 773,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Sporadic lesions and lesions associated with Maffucci syndrome are associated with IDH1 p.R132C hotspot mutations {23485734; 22057234; 28845532}. Frequent somatic alterations in 2p22.3, 2q24.3, and 14q11.2 are also found in spindle cell haemangiomas and enchondromas occurring in patients with Maffucci syndrome {25565925}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T12:00:29"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 837,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 780,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127794,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 252,
        "headingId": 780,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>See <em>Clinical features</em>, above.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T12:00:29"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 831,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 774,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127795,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 252,
        "headingId": 774,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The tumours are relatively well circumscribed, often partially intravascular, or sometimes poorly defined. They occupy the dermis, with frequent extension into the subcutis, and they consist of irregular, dilated, thin-walled, congested, cavernous-like vascular channels mixed with solid areas of vacuolated epithelioid endothelial cells scattered among bland spindle-shaped cells with pericytic and fibroblastic features and poorly defined eosinophilic cytoplasm. The cavernous vascular channels are lined by a single layer of bland endothelial cells. Thrombi, with or without dystrophic calcification, are common. Smooth muscle cells may be seen focally. Degenerative cytological atypia is sometimes present. The endothelial cells within the vascular channels and the epithelioid vacuolated cells show positive immunostaining for endothelial markers. Spindle cells may show focal positivity for SMA {2071088; 23485734; 8827025; 3740350}.</p>\r\n<h6>Differential diagnosis</h6>\r\n<p>Nodular Kaposi sarcoma lacks cavernous vascular channels and epithelioid vacuolated cells and is positive for KSHV/HHV8.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T12:00:29"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 832,
    "bookid": 64,
